e="author"><name><surname>Shimeld</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>The evolution of the hedgehog gene family in chordates: Insights from amphioxus hedgehog.</article-title><source>Dev Genes Evol</source><volume>209</volume><fpage>40</fpage><lpage>47</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9914417</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000864-Methot1"><label>7</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Methot</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Basler</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>An absolute requirement for Cubitus interruptus in Hedgehog signaling.</article-title><source>Development</source><volume>128</volume><fpage>733</fpage><lpage>742</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11171398</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000864-RuiziAltaba1"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ruiz i Altaba</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>The works of GLI and the power of hedgehog.</article-title><source>Nat Cell Biol</source><volume>1</volume><fpage>E147</fpage><lpage>148</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10559974</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000864-Hui1"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hui</surname><given-names>CC</given-names></name><name><surname>Slusarski</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Platt</surname><given-names>KA</given-names></name><name><surname>Holmgren</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Joyner</surname><given-names>AL</given-names></name></person-group><year>1994</year><article-title>Expression of three mouse homologs of the Drosophila segment polarity gene cubitus interruptus, Gli, Gli-2, and Gli-3, in ectoderm- and mesoderm-derived tissues suggests multiple roles during postimplantation development.</article-title><source>Dev Biol</source><volume>162</volume><fpage>402</fpage><lpage>413</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8150204</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000864-Ruppert1"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ruppert</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Kinzler</surname><given-names>KW</given-names></name><name><surname>Wong</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Bigner</surname><given-names>SH</given-names></name><name><surname>Kao</surname><given-names>FT</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1988</year><article-title>The GLI-Kruppel family of human genes.</article-title><source>Mol Cell Biol</source><volume>8</volume><fpage>3104</fpage><lpage>3113</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2850480</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000864-Wang1"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Fallon</surname><given-names>JF</given-names></name><name><surname>Beachy</surname><given-names>PA</given-names></name></person-group><year>2000</year><article-title>Hedgehog-regulated processing of Gli3 produces an anterior/posterior repressor gradient in the developing vertebrate limb.</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>100</volume><fpage>423</fpage><lpage>434</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10693759</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000864-Pan1"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pan</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Bai</surname><given-names>CB</given-names></name><name><surname>Joyner</surname><given-names>AL</given-names></name><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Sonic hedgehog signaling regulates Gli2 transcriptional activity by suppressing its processing and degradation.</article-title><source>Mol Cell Biol</source><volume>26</volume><fpage>3365</fpage><lpage>3377</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16611981</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000864-Stamataki1"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Stamataki</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Ulloa</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Tsoni</surname><given-names>SV</given-names></name><name><surname>Mynett</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Briscoe</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>A gradient of Gli activity mediates graded Sonic Hedgehog signaling in the neural tube.</article-title><source>Genes Dev</source><volume>19</volume><fpage>626</fpage><lpage>641</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15741323</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000864-AzaBlanc1"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Aza-Blanc</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>H-Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Ruiz i Altaba</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Kornberg</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>2000</year><article-title>Expression of vertebrate Gli proteins in Drosophila reveals a distribution of activator and repressor activities.</article-title><source>Development</source><volume>127</volume><fpage>4293</fpage><lpage>4301</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10976059</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000864-Mering1"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mering</surname><given-names>Cv</given-names></name><name><surname>Basler</surname><given-names>K<